From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kevin Turcios <turcioskevinr@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 03:12:26 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Resolve with nab-resolver behind --resolver=nab

nab-resolver, vendored beside resolvelib, is a PubGrub solver. With
--resolver=nab it searches for the answer in place of resolvelib;
everything around the search -- pip's factory, candidates, requirements,
provider, the RequirementSet it builds and the errors it reports -- is
pip's, as with resolvelib. --resolver is kpip's own option rather than a
--use-feature value: kpip shares pip's configuration, and pip ignores a key
it does not know but rejects a value it does not know for one it does.
Build dependencies resolve with the same resolver.

Each of pip's requirements is the range of what could be chosen while it
is in play: what pip's finder offers for it, pre-releases included, and
what a requirement seen admits beside it (a direct URL, a pinned yanked
release); a range widens when one is learnt, by starting again. Which of
those pip would offer depends on everything in play, so nab chooses a
version by asking pip about the requirements of the user and of the
candidates it has decided on, as resolvelib asks, and taking pip's first
candidate in nab's range: pip's pre-release, yanked, direct-URL and
installed-version rules, and its order, hold. Where nab has derived a range
before what admits it is in play, a requirement seen from a parent version
it has not ruled out may admit it; nothing else does.
A version so chosen must be
one pip offers beside the answer's own requirements, or the package is
chosen again without it. A version pip finds
is one choice, built from the first of its files that builds.

As pip, nab reads no index for an installed version pip prefers: that
version is its range until a requirement on it reads the index, or a
resolution fails without the index, and then it starts again with it.

A failure is reported through the requirements nab's derivation names, in
pip's words.
